Careful with malformed semantics: For a row whose tax_id is unparseable, we can't know if it matches; safest is fail closed for the whole lookup (return null). Because a malformed row might be the requested record. So any malformed row → null. That's strict and matches "malformed record ... berarti unavailable".

Row parse: tax_id must be a safe integer nonzero; rate must be a string; tax_out must be string or null/undefined. Only for the matching id do we strictly canonicalize rate (parseProviderTaxRatePercent) — but spec says malformed record → unavailable; if a non-matching row has rate "abc", is it malformed? It cannot affect our tax. But strictness is simpler: validate shape for all rows (tax_id integer, rate string, tax_out string|null), and canonicalize rate only for match. Negative rate rows are legitimate master data (PPH 23) so canonicalization (which rejects negative) must only apply to the matched record. Good.

`outputAccount`: tax_out string non-empty trimmed → value; null/undefined/"" → null.

Duplicate detection: if two rows share requested tax_id → null.

Stop conditions: if fetched >= totalCount → stop. If page returns empty data and fetched < totalCount → stop (avoid infinite loop) and if no match → null.

Also: a match found but totalCount not exhausted → keep scanning to detect duplicates. That's what "harus tepat satu" requires.

Rate canonicalization: return canonical string (e.g. "11").

